the hellenistic are different from the  classical largely in the greater realism  and the motion our title power and  pathos the power has to do with  political power cultural power in the  wake of Alexander and pathos is really  the Greek for lived experience the  Hellenistic is the age where we get  vivid portraiture realistic expressions  and images of people where we can almost  look past their faces very natural forms  and think we see into their souls on  what they're thinking and this is the  really the art of portraiture being born  in this period politically individuals  lost power there were no longer  democracies functioning controlling  their own city life at Athens or Sparta  there were distant rulers the successors  of Alexander the Great and people seemed  to have turned inward in philosophy  others to religion but also in art going  for local honors making statues of  themselves their family members and  those they wish to honor that really  focused on those individual  accomplishments whether it be in  rhetoric in philanthropy in athletics or  some other area we look at bronze in  this case as a medium that contributed  to the artistic developments to a new  styles to new forms of expression new  forms of seeing the human figure and be  it the body or faces in this period  whence alexander conquers all of western  asia  we have a society that's not the similar  to our own global in the sense of pan  mediterranean but you have Greeks Romans  Etruscans people from the ancient Near  East all talking to each other all  moving around traveling and trading many  of the statues in the show twenty  percent of them were found in shipwrecks  and that's not surprising because in  this period there was now an art market  and statues were being transported  overseas this show is in the  extraordinary gathering of about 50  ancient bronze sculptures which is more  than have ever been brought together in  one place in one time in the modern  period and more than we'll probably have  ever again for a long long time  ancient bronzes were valuable and  populated the ancient world in the tens  of thousands but over the centuries most  of them have been melted down and  recycled to reuse their bronze as coins  sword shield buckets or even hinges in  antiquity bronze statues were quite  ubiquitous in public squares and  sanctuaries and places like this and if  you went to places like this you were  surrounded by them so the exhibition  gives an opportunity to maybe capture  some of that  I want visitors to really look closely  and see past the broken edges or the  hollow eyes and reconstruct in their  minds I these complete images of  individuals that are so vivid and speak  to us across time
 

